Hash Solution [Brute Force]
Approach:
Create temp to traverse 
Create an unordered_set<ListNode*> to store the addresses of visited nodes (hashing).
[unordered_set stores only unique node pointers (addresses). It does not store frequencies.]
Traverse the linked list
If the current node is already present in the hash set, a cycle exists.
If no return false 
Insert temp in nodeSet and move temp to next 
Why to insert - to check if is it in linked list or not 

If there is a cycle, the fast pointer moves one extra node compared to the slow pointer in each iteration. Eventually, it catches up to the slow pointer inside the loop.


Time Complexity: O(N) 
HashSet - O(1) ---[O(1) * O(N)]

Space Complexity: O(N)
As HashSet stores that visited to that node 

Code:

class Solution {
public:
    bool hasCycle(ListNode *head) {
        ListNode* temp=head;
        std::unordered_set<ListNode*> nodeSet;
        while(temp!=NULL){
            if(nodeSet.find(temp) != nodeSet.end()){
                return true;
            }
            nodeSet.insert(temp);
            temp=temp->next;
        }
        return false;

    }
};

Floyd's Algorithm [Tortoise and Hare algo]
Approach:
Create two pointers : Slow and Fast 
Slow moves one node at a time 
Fast moves two node at a time 
So treverse till fast is not equal to null or fast->next is null
nd move slow and fast 
if loop is there 
slow and fast will be at a same point 
then return true 
or else return false 

Time Complexity: O(N)
At each step, decreased gap between slow and fast by one node

Space Complexity: O(1)
It utilizes constant amount of additional space 

Code: 

class Solution {
public:
    bool hasCycle(ListNode *head) {
        ListNode* slow=head;
        ListNode* fast=head;
        while(fast!=NULL && fast->next!=NULL){
            slow=slow->next;
            fast=fast->next->next;
        
        if(slow==fast){
            return true;
        }
        }
        return false;

    }
};



